-- Q1: easy / L1 — ASC 260 scope: which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which of the following entities is required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["A publicly traded corporation with common shares listed on a major exchange", "A privately held partnership with no publicly traded debt", "A sole proprietorship filing audited financial statements", "A private company reporting under the modified cash basis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required for entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock. Private companies, partnerships, and sole proprietorships are not required to present EPS because they do not have publicly traded equity. Choice B is incorrect because a partnership without publicly traded securities has no obligation to present per-share data under ASC 260.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Definition of potential common shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which of the following best defines potential common shares?', '["Shares held in treasury that can be reissued at any time", "Securities or contracts that may entitle the holder to obtain common stock", "Authorized but unissued shares available for future offerings", "Shares owned by insiders subject to a lock-up period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, potential common shares are securities or other contracts that may entitle the holder to obtain common stock during the reporting period, such as stock options, warrants, and convertible securities. This definition drives the diluted EPS computation. Choice A is incorrect because treasury shares are already-issued shares repurchased by the company and are not potential common shares.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Simple vs complex capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, an entity has a simple capital structure when it has which characteristic?', '["Fewer than 100,000 common shares outstanding", "Common stock listed on a national securities exchange", "No outstanding securities that could potentially dilute basic EPS", "Only one class of preferred stock in addition to common stock"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-3, a simple capital structure exists when an entity has no outstanding potential common shares (such as options, warrants, or convertible securities) that could dilute earnings per share. An entity with a simple capital structure presents only basic EPS. Choice A is incorrect because the distinction depends on the presence of dilutive securities, not the number of shares outstanding.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Evaluate two-class method for participating preferred
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redstone Holdings Inc. has 500,000 common shares and 100,000 shares of Series A preferred stock outstanding. The Series A preferred receives a $2 per share dividend and participates equally in undistributed earnings with common shareholders. Net income is $1,800,000 and declared preferred dividends total $200,000. The CFO computed basic EPS as $3.20 without using the two-class method. Evaluate the correct approach and determine basic EPS under the two-class method.', '["$3.20, because the two-class method is optional for participating securities", "$3.60, because participating preferred shares are excluded from the denominator", "$2.93, because undistributed earnings are allocated only to common shareholders after deducting preferred dividends", "$2.67, because the two-class method allocates undistributed earnings to both common and participating preferred"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method is required when participating securities exist. Undistributed earnings of $1,600,000 ($1,800,000 less $200,000 preferred dividends) are allocated 5/6 to common ($1,333,333) based on the 500,000/600,000 share ratio, producing basic EPS of $2.67. Choice A ($3.20) incorrectly ignores the required allocation of undistributed earnings to the participating preferred stock.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Effect of mid-year share buyback on EPS trend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Waverly Corp. reported net income of $2,000,000 in both Year 1 and Year 2, with 400,000 shares outstanding all of Year 1. On July 1 of Year 2, Waverly repurchased 100,000 shares. What is the most likely effect of the repurchase on Year 2 basic EPS compared to Year 1?', '["Basic EPS increases from $5.00 to $5.71 because the weighted-average shares decrease to 350,000", "Basic EPS remains at $5.00 because the repurchase is not retroactively applied to weighted-average shares", "Basic EPS decreases from $5.00 to $4.00 because all 100,000 repurchased shares are removed for the full year", "Basic EPS increases from $5.00 to $6.67 because only shares outstanding at year-end are used in the calculation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares repurchased are excluded from the weighted average from the repurchase date forward. Waverly''s Year 2 weighted average is 350,000 shares (400,000 for six months plus 300,000 for six months), producing EPS of $5.71 versus Year 1''s $5.00. Choice C incorrectly removes all 100,000 shares for the entire year rather than only from the July 1 repurchase date forward.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Mandatorily convertible instruments
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinecrest Industries Inc. issued $2,500,000 of mandatorily convertible bonds on January 1, 2025, with an 8% coupon and mandatory conversion into 100,000 common shares on December 31, 2027. The tax rate is 25%. Current-year net income is $3,000,000 with 500,000 weighted-average common shares and no preferred dividends. The CFO is evaluating whether these bonds affect diluted EPS before the mandatory conversion date. Evaluate the correct treatment and compute diluted EPS.', '["$6.00, because mandatorily convertible bonds are excluded from diluted EPS until actual conversion occurs", "$5.25, because the if-converted method adds back after-tax interest and includes the potential shares", "$6.30, because only the after-tax interest is added to the numerator without additional shares in the denominator", "$5.00, because the potential shares are added to the denominator without adjusting the numerator for interest"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, mandatorily convertible securities are included in diluted EPS before conversion using the if-converted method. After-tax interest of $150,000 ($200,000 x 0.75) is added to the numerator, and 100,000 potential shares are added to the denominator, yielding $5.25 ($3,150,000 / 600,000). Choice D ($5.00) incorrectly adds shares without removing the interest charge from the numerator, overstating the dilutive effect.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — If-converted method: after-tax interest add-back for convertible bonds
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Brookfield Manufacturing Corp. has net income of $1,500,000, 400,000 weighted-average common shares, and $2,000,000 face value of 5% convertible bonds that convert into 50,000 shares. The tax rate is 25%. What is diluted EPS under the if-converted method?', '["$3.75", "$3.56", "$3.50", "$3.33"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method requires adding after-tax interest ($100,000 x 0.75 = $75,000) to the numerator and the 50,000 convertible shares to the denominator, producing diluted EPS of $3.50 ($1,575,000 / 450,000). Choice D ($3.33) incorrectly adds the potential shares to the denominator without adding back the after-tax interest savings to the numerator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Retroactive EPS adjustment for post-period stock splits
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, when a stock split occurs after the balance sheet date but before the financial statements are issued, how is EPS affected?', '["EPS is calculated using pre-split shares for all periods presented", "EPS is adjusted only for the current period using post-split shares", "No adjustment is required until the following fiscal year", "EPS is retroactively adjusted for all periods presented using post-split shares"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statement issuance require retroactive adjustment of EPS for all periods presented to ensure comparability. This treatment also applies to stock dividends. Choice A is incorrect because using pre-split shares would overstate EPS and undermine the comparability that ASC 260 requires.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: hard / L3 — Loss from continuing operations as control number for dilution
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Alderton Enterprises Inc. reports a loss from continuing operations of $800,000 and income from discontinued operations (net of tax) of $1,200,000, producing net income of $400,000. The company has 200,000 weighted-average shares and 10,000 in-the-money stock options that would add 3,000 incremental shares under the treasury stock method. Analyze how the loss from continuing operations affects the diluted EPS computation for all line items.', '["The stock options are excluded from diluted EPS for all line items because including them would be antidilutive to the continuing operations loss", "The stock options are included for all line items because overall net income is positive at $400,000", "The stock options are included only for the discontinued operations line item", "The stock options are included for continuing operations but excluded for discontinued operations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, the control number for determining whether potential shares are dilutive is income from continuing operations, not net income. Because Alderton reports a loss from continuing operations, including the options would reduce the loss per share, which is antidilutive. This exclusion applies to all EPS line items. Choice B is incorrect because the positive net income figure does not override the continuing operations control number.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Definition of weighted-average shares outstanding
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what does the term weighted-average common shares outstanding represent?', '["The total number of authorized common shares at year-end", "The number of shares weighted by the portion of the reporting period they were outstanding", "The average of beginning and ending common shares regardless of transaction timing", "The maximum number of shares outstanding at any point during the reporting period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, weighted-average common shares outstanding are computed by weighting the number of shares by the fraction of the period they were outstanding. This approach ensures EPS reflects the capital actually available during the period. Choice C is incorrect because a simple average of beginning and ending shares fails to capture the precise timing of share issuances or repurchases within the period.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Anti-dilution sequencing with multiple convertible instruments
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Cedarbrook Industries Inc. has basic EPS of $3.00 based on $900,000 net income and 300,000 shares. It has three potentially dilutive securities: (1) stock options adding 8,000 incremental shares with no numerator effect, (2) $200,000 of 5% convertible bonds at a 25% tax rate converting into 12,000 shares, and (3) convertible preferred stock with $45,000 in dividends converting into 20,000 shares. Evaluate the correct order for testing dilution under ASC 260.', '["Options, preferred, bonds — ranked by number of incremental shares from largest to smallest", "Bonds, preferred, options — ranked by face value of the underlying security", "Options, bonds, preferred — ranked by lowest to highest per-share incremental impact on EPS", "Preferred, bonds, options — ranked by highest to lowest per-share incremental impact on EPS"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, potentially dilutive securities must be ranked from most dilutive to least dilutive (lowest to highest per-share impact) when testing for inclusion. Options have $0.00 per share, bonds have $0.625 ($7,500 / 12,000), and preferred has $2.25 ($45,000 / 20,000). Choice D reverses the required sequence, which could prematurely exclude a dilutive security.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Written put options and reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glenwood Corp. has 300,000 shares outstanding and wrote put options obligating it to repurchase 15,000 shares at $50 each. The average market price during the period was $40. Analyze the effect of the written put options on diluted EPS under ASC 260.', '["The put options have no effect because they are out-of-the-money", "15,000 shares are added to the denominator as potential common shares", "3,000 incremental shares are added using the standard treasury stock method", "3,750 incremental shares are added because the exercise price exceeds the average market price"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the average market price. Using the reverse treasury stock method, Glenwood would issue 18,750 shares ($750,000 / $40) to fund the $750,000 repurchase obligation for 15,000 shares, adding 3,750 net incremental shares. Choice A is incorrect because written puts are dilutive (not out-of-the-money) precisely when the exercise price exceeds the market price.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Weighted-average shares with shares issued in business combination
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fairfield Industries Corp. had 250,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On September 1, 2025, Fairfield issued 60,000 shares to acquire Eastmont Corp. in a business combination. What is Fairfield''s weighted-average number of common shares for 2025?', '["270,000", "280,000", "310,000", "250,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued in a business combination are included in the weighted average from the acquisition date. The 60,000 shares issued September 1 are weighted for 4/12 of the year, adding 20,000 shares to the base 250,000 for a total of 270,000. Choice C (310,000) incorrectly includes the acquisition shares as if they were outstanding for the entire year.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Reverse stock split effect on comparative EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Summit Resources Corp. reported basic EPS of $2.00 in Year 1, based on net income of $600,000 and 300,000 shares. In Year 2, Summit executes a 1-for-3 reverse stock split on January 1 and reports net income of $500,000. Analyze the comparative EPS presentation for both years.', '["Year 1: $2.00; Year 2: $5.00", "Year 1: $6.00; Year 2: $5.00", "Year 1: $2.00; Year 2: $1.67", "Year 1: $6.00; Year 2: $15.00"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, a reverse stock split must be applied retroactively to all prior periods presented. Year 1 shares are restated from 300,000 to 100,000, producing restated EPS of $6.00 ($600,000 / 100,000). Year 2 EPS is $5.00 ($500,000 / 100,000). Choice A fails to retroactively adjust Year 1, destroying the period-to-period comparability required by ASC 260.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Basic EPS with mid-year treasury stock repurchase
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Haverford Industries Inc. had 400,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On October 1, the company repurchased 100,000 shares as treasury stock. Net income was $1,500,000 with no preferred dividends. What is Haverford''s basic EPS for 2025?', '["$3.75", "$5.00", "$4.00", "$4.29"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, treasury shares are excluded from the weighted average from the repurchase date forward. Haverford''s weighted-average shares are 375,000 (400,000 x 9/12 + 300,000 x 3/12), yielding basic EPS of $4.00 ($1,500,000 / 375,000). Choice B ($5.00) incorrectly uses only the 300,000 year-end shares rather than weighting shares over the entire reporting period.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Warrants in treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Technologies Corp. has 500,000 weighted-average shares and 20,000 outstanding warrants with an exercise price of $30 per share. The average market price during 2025 was $50. Using the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are added to the denominator for diluted EPS?', '["20,000", "12,000", "5,000", "8,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes warrants are exercised and the $600,000 in proceeds (20,000 x $30) are used to repurchase shares at the average market price of $50, buying back 12,000 shares. The incremental 8,000 shares (20,000 issued minus 12,000 repurchased) are added to the denominator. Choice A (20,000) incorrectly adds all exercised shares without applying the treasury stock repurchase assumption.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Required EPS line items on income statement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which line items on the income statement require per-share disclosure for entities with complex capital structures?', '["Only net income per share", "Gross profit per share and net income per share", "Operating income per share and income before tax per share", "Income from continuing operations per share and net income per share"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, entities must present both basic and diluted EPS for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. If discontinued operations are reported, EPS for that component is also required. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 does not require per-share amounts for operating income or income before tax.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Analyze whether convertible preferred is dilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakridge Holdings Corp. reports net income of $2,000,000 with 400,000 weighted-average common shares and 50,000 shares of $100 par, 5% convertible preferred stock. Each preferred share converts into 2 common shares, and preferred dividends of $250,000 were declared. Basic EPS is $4.375. Analyze whether the convertible preferred is dilutive.', '["The preferred is dilutive because its per-share impact of $2.50 is less than basic EPS of $4.375", "The preferred is antidilutive because conversion would decrease the numerator by $250,000", "The preferred is dilutive only if the conversion ratio exceeds three common shares per preferred share", "The preferred is antidilutive because the $250,000 dividend add-back exceeds the incremental shares'' impact"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, convertible preferred stock is tested using the if-converted method. The per-share impact is $2.50 ($250,000 dividend add-back / 100,000 incremental shares), which is below basic EPS of $4.375, confirming the conversion is dilutive and reduces EPS to $4.00. Choice D is incorrect because the test compares per-share impact to basic EPS, not the absolute dollar amount of dividends.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L2 — Contingently issuable shares when conditions not met
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ferndale Corp. has 200,000 shares outstanding and an agreement to issue 25,000 additional shares if cumulative revenues exceed $50 million by December 31, 2025. At year-end, cumulative revenues total $42 million. How should the contingent shares be treated for basic EPS?', '["Include 25,000 shares in the denominator because the agreement exists", "Exclude the 25,000 shares because the revenue condition has not been met at the reporting date", "Include a pro-rata portion of 21,000 shares based on revenue progress toward the target", "Include 25,000 shares only if revenues are projected to meet the target in the next fiscal year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS only when all necessary conditions have been satisfied at the end of the reporting period. Because Ferndale''s cumulative revenues of $42 million have not met the $50 million threshold, the 25,000 shares are excluded. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 does not permit pro-rata inclusion based on partial progress toward meeting a contingency.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Diluted EPS with convertible preferred using if-converted method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashland Holdings Corp. has net income of $1,800,000, 500,000 weighted-average common shares, and 30,000 shares of cumulative preferred stock with an annual dividend of $4 per share. Each preferred share converts into 3 common shares. What is diluted EPS using the if-converted method?', '["$3.36", "$3.20", "$3.05", "$2.85"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method assumes preferred stock was converted at the beginning of the period. The numerator uses full net income of $1,800,000 (preferred dividends of $120,000 are not subtracted), and the denominator includes 90,000 additional shares (30,000 x 3), yielding diluted EPS of $3.05 ($1,800,000 / 590,000). Choice D ($2.85) incorrectly deducts preferred dividends while also adding the converted shares, double-counting the preferred stock''s impact.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Two-class method: undistributed earnings allocation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitfield Inc. has 800,000 common shares and 200,000 shares of Series B participating preferred stock. The Series B preferred receives a $1.50 per share dividend before participating equally with common in all additional distributions. Net income is $2,500,000, with $300,000 declared to preferred and $800,000 declared to common. Evaluate the allocation of undistributed earnings under the two-class method to determine basic EPS.', '["$2.75", "$3.13", "$1.40", "$2.40"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method allocates undistributed earnings of $1,400,000 ($2,500,000 less $1,100,000 total dividends) proportionally: common receives 80% ($1,120,000) based on 800,000 / 1,000,000. Adding distributed dividends of $800,000 yields a common numerator of $1,920,000 and basic EPS of $2.40. Choice A ($2.75) incorrectly applies the traditional approach, subtracting only preferred dividends without allocating undistributed earnings to the participating preferred.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Performance-based stock options effect on diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Sterling Analytics Inc. has 400,000 weighted-average shares and granted 20,000 performance-based stock options with an exercise price of $25 that vest only if annual revenue exceeds $20 million. Current-year revenue is $18 million, and the average market price is $40. Analyze how these options affect diluted EPS.', '["The options are excluded because the performance condition is not satisfied at the reporting date", "The options are included and add 7,500 incremental shares via the treasury stock method", "The options add 20,000 shares because performance vesting conditions are ignored for diluted EPS", "The options are included at a reduced amount proportional to revenue progress toward the target"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, stock options with performance conditions are included in diluted EPS only if the conditions would be satisfied as of the reporting date. Because Sterling''s revenue of $18 million falls short of the $20 million threshold, the options are excluded from the diluted EPS computation entirely. Choice B incorrectly applies the treasury stock method to options whose vesting conditions have not been met.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Stock dividend retroactive treatment on weighted-average shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestwood Corp. had 300,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On August 1, the company declared and distributed a 20% stock dividend. Net income is $1,440,000 with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS for 2025?', '["$4.80", "$4.00", "$4.43", "$3.60"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, stock dividends are applied retroactively as if they occurred at the beginning of the earliest period presented. Crestwood''s entire year uses 360,000 shares (300,000 x 1.20), producing basic EPS of $4.00 ($1,440,000 / 360,000). Choice C ($4.43) incorrectly treats the stock dividend like a cash share issuance by weighting the additional 60,000 shares only from August 1 forward.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Interim-period EPS computation rules
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hartwell Corp. reports quarterly basic EPS of $1.10 in Q1, $0.80 in Q2, $1.50 in Q3, and $0.90 in Q4. An analyst sums these figures to report full-year basic EPS of $4.30. Analyze whether this approach complies with ASC 260.', '["The approach is correct because EPS is an additive metric across interim periods", "The approach is correct only if each quarter used the same weighted-average share count", "The approach is incorrect because full-year EPS must be computed independently using annual figures", "The approach is incorrect only if a stock split occurred during the year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2 and ASC 270-10-45-11, full-year EPS must be computed using full-year weighted-average shares and full-year income, not by summing interim EPS amounts. Because weighted-average shares may differ each quarter due to issuances or repurchases, the sum of quarterly EPS generally will not equal independently computed annual EPS. Choice A is incorrect because EPS is not an additive metric across periods with varying share counts.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — At-the-money options produce zero incremental shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakeview Resources Corp. has 600,000 weighted-average shares and 15,000 stock options with an exercise price of $35. The average market price during 2025 was $35. Using the treasury stock method, what is the effect on diluted EPS?', '["15,000 incremental shares are added to the denominator", "7,500 incremental shares are added to the denominator", "Zero incremental shares are added because the exercise price equals the average market price", "The options are classified as antidilutive and must be disclosed in the footnotes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes proceeds from exercise ($525,000) are used to repurchase shares at the average market price ($35), resulting in 15,000 shares repurchased — exactly equal to the 15,000 shares issued. The net incremental shares are zero, producing no effect on diluted EPS. Choice A incorrectly adds all exercised shares without applying the treasury stock repurchase assumption.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Basic EPS formula numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, the numerator for the basic EPS calculation equals which of the following?', '["Net income plus preferred dividends", "Net income divided by common shares issued during the year", "Operating income less preferred dividends", "Net income less dividends on preferred stock"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, the numerator for basic EPS is net income less preferred dividends (whether declared for noncumulative preferred or accumulated for cumulative preferred), representing income available to common shareholders. Choice A is incorrect because preferred dividends are subtracted from, not added to, net income when computing the basic EPS numerator.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Cumulative preferred dividends in arrears
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Kensington Holdings Corp. has net income of $2,400,000 and 600,000 weighted-average common shares. The company has 40,000 shares of $100 par, 5% cumulative preferred stock. Preferred dividends have not been declared for the current year or the prior year. What is basic EPS for the current year?', '["$3.67", "$3.33", "$4.00", "$3.00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, only the current period''s cumulative preferred dividend of $200,000 (40,000 x $100 x 5%) is deducted from the basic EPS numerator, regardless of whether it was declared. Prior-year arrearages are not deducted again because they were already subtracted in the year they accrued. Choice B ($3.33) incorrectly deducts two years of dividends, double-counting the prior-year arrearage.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Definition of participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260-10-20, which of the following best describes a participating security?', '["A security that must be converted into common stock by a specified date", "A security that may participate in undistributed earnings with common stock", "A security issued to employees that vests based on service conditions", "A security that carries voting rights equal to those of common stock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, a participating security is one that may participate in undistributed earnings with common stock, whether that participation is conditional or unconditional. This classification triggers the two-class method for computing basic EPS. Choice A describes a mandatorily convertible security, which is a different category of instrument and is not inherently a participating security.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Mid-year bond conversion effect on diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Corp. has net income of $3,000,000 and 500,000 weighted-average common shares (which already include 25,000 converted shares from July 1 onward). On July 1, 2025, bondholders converted $1,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds into 25,000 shares. The tax rate is 25%. Analyze how the mid-year conversion affects the diluted EPS computation.', '["$5.85, because no interest adjustment is needed after actual conversion", "$6.00, using only the basic weighted-average share count", "$5.90, because the if-converted method adds pre-conversion after-tax interest and incremental shares for January through June", "$5.80, because a full year of after-tax interest and shares must be added regardless of conversion date"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method assumes conversion occurred at the start of the period. For the pre-conversion period (January through June), after-tax interest of $22,500 ($30,000 x 0.75) is added to the numerator and 12,500 incremental shares (25,000 x 6/12) are added to the denominator, yielding diluted EPS of $5.90 ($3,022,500 / 512,500). Choice D ($5.80) incorrectly uses a full year of interest and shares, ignoring that the converted shares are already actual shares from July 1 onward.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Contracts settleable in shares or cash
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northgate Corp. has 500,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $2,500,000. The company has an outstanding forward contract to sell 30,000 of its own shares at $60 each. The average market price during 2025 was $80. The controller must evaluate whether this contract affects diluted EPS and which settlement assumption applies. Evaluate the correct treatment under ASC 260.', '["The contract adds 7,500 incremental shares to the denominator because share settlement is assumed when the effect is dilutive", "The contract is excluded because it will be settled in cash at the company''s option", "The contract adds 30,000 shares because the full delivery obligation must be included in the denominator", "The contract is antidilutive because the contractual price of $60 is below the average market price of $80"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-36, contracts that may be settled in shares or cash are presumed to be settled in shares when the effect is dilutive. Applying the treasury stock method, Northgate issues 30,000 shares and uses the $1,800,000 proceeds to repurchase 22,500 shares at $80, yielding 7,500 net incremental shares. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 requires the more dilutive settlement assumption, not the assumption that favors the reporting entity.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
